@node Extending emacsctl
@section Extending emacsctl

The extension points, cheapest first:

@subsection A command bound to a D-Bus method (one table row)

Add a row to the right spec table (@file{ctl-cmd-editor.c},
@file{ctl-cmd-subsys.c}, or @file{ctl-cmd-gowl.c}):

@example
@{ "gsurf mute", "Mute the page",
  CTL_IFACE_GSURF, "Mute", "s?:buffer", CTL_REPLY_STRING @},
@end example

Argspec tokens: @code{s} string, @code{o} object path,
@code{i}/@code{u} int32/uint32, @code{x}/@code{t} int64/uint64,
@code{d} double, @code{b} bool, @code{?} marks optional,
@code{D:name} consumes trailing @code{KEY=VALUE} pairs as
@code{a@{ss@}}.  Reply kinds: @code{STRING}, @code{JSON},
@code{BOOL}, @code{INT}, @code{STRLIST}, @code{EXIT_OUTPUT},
@code{NONE}.  Usage text, @code{help} listing, all four output
formats and group completion derive automatically.

@subsection An alias of a registry tool

When the capability is a tool in the registry (a
@code{cmacs-brigade-deftool}, @pxref{Capability Table}), the verb should
not name a D-Bus method of its own: @samp{emacsctl tool GROUP VERB}
already reaches it.  Give it a short positional spelling with one row in
@code{alias_specs} in @file{ctl-cmd-alias.c}, and put
@code{CTL_ALIAS_ROW ("the verb")} where the verb belongs in its group's
table:

@example
/* ctl-cmd-alias.c */
@{ "gsurf_set_zoom", "level buffer", NULL, CTL_ALIAS_TEXT, 0,
  @{ "gsurf zoom", "Set the zoom level",
    CTL_IFACE_GSURF, "SetZoom", "d:level s?:buffer",
    CTL_REPLY_STRING @} @},

/* ctl-cmd-subsys.c, among the gsurf rows */
CTL_ALIAS_ROW ("gsurf zoom"),
@end example

The first line is the tool, the tool parameter each positional becomes,
defaults for positionals left out (@code{NULL} or @code{"name=value
..."}), how to print the result (@code{CTL_ALIAS_TEXT}, @code{_JSON},
@code{_PRINTED}, @code{_PRINTED_LINES}) and the tool's @code{:timeout}
in seconds when it is @code{:async} (else 0).  The inner row is an
ordinary method row: its argspec types the positionals, and its D-Bus
method is what the verb calls against a cmacs that cannot serve the
tool.  A tool with no hand-written D-Bus method does not need an alias
--- it is already @samp{emacsctl tool ...}.

Only tools on the @code{cli} surface can be aliased (a destructive tool
is not on it unless its @code{:surfaces} says so).  @samp{make -C
cmacs/emacsctl check} refuses a malformed row, and
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-emacsctl-alias-tests.el} refuses one whose tool,
parameters or types do not match the generated capability reference.  A
verb that stays hand-written needs a line in @code{kept_verbs} in the
same file saying why; the unit tests refuse a verb with neither.

@subsection A local or composite command

@code{ctl_simple_command_new (name, summary, usage, callback)} plus
one @code{ctl_command_registry_add()} call in
@code{ctl_builtin_register_all()}.  For per-command flags or custom
completion, subclass @code{CtlCommand} and override the vtable.

@subsection A REPL language

One-method-per-eval languages are a @code{CtlReplSpec} row in
@file{ctl-repl.c} plus one @code{ctl_repl_runtime_register()} call;
richer languages subclass @code{CtlReplRuntime} and implement
@code{is_complete} / @code{eval} themselves.

@subsection An output format

Implement the one-method @code{CtlFormatter} interface (copy
@file{ctl-formatter-raw.c}) and add a branch to
@code{ctl_formatter_for_name()}.

@subsection A transport

Implement @code{CtlTransport} (@code{call}, @code{subscribe},
@code{unsubscribe}, @code{list_instances}, @code{close}; emit
@code{closed} on drop) and construct it in
@code{ctl_invocation_get_transport()}.  Everything above the
interface is transport-blind.

@subsection Keeping the server in sync

A new capability is written once, as a tool in the registry; it is then
an MCP tool, a @code{Tools.Call} target and an @samp{emacsctl tool} verb
with no further work, and nothing has to be kept in sync.

For what is still hand-written the old discipline holds: a new MCP tool
in @file{cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools-*.c} gets a matching typed method in
the paired @file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-*.c} (and vice versa), and
a new D-Bus method gets its emacsctl table row.  Each file's header
comment names its partner.  @samp{admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh
--list cli} lists every verb with where it comes from.
